> (I assume you can't wish for a corpse if you've genocided them.

I don't think so.  But the corpses are, if not quite as dangerous
as the live article, not very far shy of it, IMO.

> But even if it turns out you can, I'll let them live. I haven't gotten
> killed by a live one for ages.)

The last time I got killed by one, that I remember, it was
in Mine's End, and I don't really see how I could have
prevented it.  The Mines had a large number of dark levels
that game, and my oil lamp had run out SEVERAL levels
earlier.  I unlocked one of the secret doors, and a yellow
c stepped out of the darkness, stood next to me, bit me,
touched me, and hissed at me.  I got zero chances to
act between seeing it for the first time and feeling
myself start to stiffen.  I got *one* move after that.
Normally I'd have eaten the lizard corpse I always keep
in main inventory, but in this game no lizards had even
been generated (unless my dog killed and ate them
entirely off-screen without my knowledge, which is
possible).  I'd seen some acidic things, but not for a
couple of levels and the corpses had long since rotted
away.

Admittedly, that's an unusual case.  Getting that far
into the game without seeing any lizards is fairly rare.

A much more common case is like in my current
game, when in the midst of a battle I got hit with
a cockatrice corpse and started to stiffen, and it's
a good thing I was paying fairly close attention to
the messages, because if I'd done something else
for a turn or so first instead of eating the lizard
corpse right away, it could easily have been
curtains for me.

So, yeah, there are two morals here.  The more
specific one is "cockatrice corpses are dangerous",
but there's also the more general: "Pay attention!"

There's actually a whole list of monster types that
make you have to sit up and pay attention to every
single message every single turn.  Green slimes
are another.  Were things (all the _various_ were
things) are another.  Nymphs are another (and a
rather common one).  Virtually any unique monster.
Liches.  Mind flayers.  Certain colors of Nagas.
Umber hulks.  Gremlins at night. There are probably
others I'm forgetting.  It's tempting to genocide
the lot of them, but man, you'd sure use up a lot
of marker charges doing so!


